"""Render the completed, private NR200 result from preserved evidence tables."""
import argparse,csv,hashlib,html,json
from pathlib import Path
p=argparse.ArgumentParser();p.add_argument('root',type=Path);p.add_argument('comparison',type=Path);a=p.parse_args();r=a.root
load=lambda x:json.loads((r/'results'/x).read_text())
s=load('protein200-compacted-nt20/summary.json');z=load('protein-resolution-compacted-v2/summary.json');sim=load('protein-similarity-support-compacted-v2-nt20/summary.json');calls=list(csv.DictReader((r/'results/protein-similarity-support-compacted-v2-nt20/sample-species-calls.tsv').open(),delimiter='\t'))
con=sim['counts']['NR_and_viral_reference_same_species'];assert con['gained_protein_call']==66 and con['human_annotated_gained_protein_call']==4
parity=json.loads((a.comparison/'full-aggregation-parity.json').read_text());assert parity['byte_identical'] and parity['pair_rows']==406173
cost=json.loads((a.comparison/'completed-search-cost-comparison.json').read_text())
def table(head,rows):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</th>' for x in head)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table>'
status=table(['NR evidence','Exact sequence pairs'],[(k,f'{v:,}') for k,v in s['nr_status'].items()])
rows=[]
for tier in ['NR_resolved_species','NR_and_viral_reference_same_species']:
 c=sim['counts'][tier];rows.append([tier,c['protein_call'],c['gained_protein_call'],c['gained_protein_call_baseline_support_0'],c['human_annotated_gained_protein_call'],c['gained_union_call']])
support=table(['Species evidence','Protein calls','Gains vs NT20','Gains from zero','Human-annotated gains','Gains when unioned with baseline'],rows)
human=table(['Sample','Frozen taxonomy name','NT20 → protein similarity support'],[['SRR37076045','Betacoronavirus pandemicum','1 → 18'],['SRR36876972','Hudisavirus sp.','2 → 4'],['SRR38294879','Enterovirus cerhino','2 → 3'],['SRR36995843','Hudisavirus sp.','2 → 3']])
text=f'''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Protein detection and NR validation: 200 samples</title><style>body{{font:17px/1.55 system-ui;max-width:1100px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 24px;color:#152333}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px}}td,th{{padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #ccd5dd;text-align:left}}h1,h2{{line-height:1.2}}.note{{background:#eef3f7;padding:18px}}a{{color:#075cab}}</style></head><body>
<h1>Protein detection and NR validation: 200 samples</h1><p>Private experiment • Results recorded 22 October 2026 • AI-authored analysis, not human-verified.</p>
<p><strong>193,505 candidate pairs have viral-only NR evidence; 96,256 are absent from the NT20 baseline retained set.</strong> Requiring agreement between the viral-reference and NR species, and at least three common sequence-similarity clusters, yields <strong>66 additional sample/species calls</strong>. Four are human-annotated threshold crossings, all from one or two baseline supports. None of those four starts from zero.</p>
<p class="note">These are experimental sequence-evidence calls. The comparison applies common sequence-similarity grouping, but does not reproduce the production alignment-coordinate duplicate stage. It is not a completed production end-to-end pipeline run, an infection diagnosis, or a known-truth sensitivity measurement.</p>
<h2>What was searched</h2><p>The frozen 200-sample subset was screened through the protein detection path against translated viral references, then its candidates were checked against the broad NR protein database. This NR experiment changes the reference used for validation. The separate core_nt experiment keeps the original nucleotide reference and tests translated searches against it. The native comparisons include NT24, NT20 and the upstream-only union; the primary figures here use NT20.</p>
<p>The 200-sample protein candidate set contains 378,939 distinct complete trimmed sequence pairs, representing 447,209 original pairs. NR was searched as a larger pooled batch of 817,744 query mates including other frozen experiment panels. DIAMOND 2.1.17 used sensitive mode, 32 threads, block size 2, four index chunks, minimum ORF 1, genetic code 1, E-value 1e-5, 30% query coverage and top 10%. Classification requires at least 25 amino acids and uses taxa within 90% of the best eligible score. Each read keeps its own evidence.</p>
{status}
<h2>Read gains and species resolution</h2><p>Of the 96,256 additional viral-only exact pairs, 77,018 have no original member retained by the NT20 initial screen. 92,592 additional pairs have viral-only evidence on both mates. Across all 193,505 viral-only pairs, 46,714 resolve to a species in NR and 28,011 agree with the viral-reference species. Among the 96,256 additions, 70,662 have an NR assignment above species; 8,430 agree at species; 14,245 disagree at species; and 2,919 have an unresolved relationship between the two references.</p>
<p>At a threshold of three common sequence-similarity clusters per sample/species:</p>{support}
<p>The stricter concordant tier has 27 gains from zero baseline support, 13 from one and 26 from two. The upstream-only union comparison also has 66 protein-only gains, four human-annotated gains and 27 from zero. The combined baseline-plus-protein union has 85 gains against NT20 and 84 against the upstream-only union. These union counts differ from requiring the protein path alone to reach three supports.</p>
<h2>The four human-annotated concordant gains</h2>{human}
<p>All four were included in the case audit, alongside ten fixed-hash selected calls. The audit includes all three or four supporting pairs for the two Hudisavirus cases and the Enterovirus case, and twelve fixed-hash pairs from the 18-support Betacoronavirus case. Raw alignments, original read IDs, reference coordinates and identities are retained.</p>
<ul><li>SRR36876972: primary amino-acid identities span 57.1–96.0%. Hits are concentrated in overlapping intervals on two references; the four supports do not establish four independent genomic loci.</li><li>SRR36995843: primary identities span 66.7–100.0%, with intervals on several references. Coordinates on different accessions cannot be treated as a common genome without alignment.</li><li>SRR38294879: primary identities span 85.7–100.0%. Two pairs map to separated intervals on KJ675505.1, while the third has a 28-amino-acid match on another accession.</li><li>SRR37076045: the twelve audited pairs span 69.2–100.0% primary identity; many mates overlap the same reference interval and many hits cluster near positions 24,200–24,500 on several accessions. These observations require duplicate/locus scrutiny before interpreting 18 similarity supports as independent evidence.</li></ul>
<h2>Common-reference coordinate inspection</h2><p>A separate local inspection maps the already audited pairs to one existing viral genome per case. Each reference was selected by the number of audited mates with near-best support, then total best score and accession. This restricted search is only for coordinates: it does not replace comprehensive validation, change calls, or supply comparable E-values.</p>
{table(['Sample','Fixed reference','Audited pairs aligned','Covered reference intervals (zero-based, half-open)','Covered bases'],[['SRR36876972','LC708018.1','4 / 4','1878–2109; 2152–2275',354],['SRR36995843','LC708016.1','3 / 3','30–168; 195–339; 1592–1739; 1778–1859; 2156–2368',722],['SRR37076045','OZ071162.1','11 / 12','23661–23745; 24237–24477',324],['SRR38294879','MZ153259.1','3 / 3','2195–2390; 3182–3266; 5960–6107',426]])}
<p>The 11 aligned Betacoronavirus pairs occupy two short regions. The Enterovirus pairs occupy three separated regions. Overlapping intervals do not determine molecular duplicates, and the Betacoronavirus audit covers twelve of eighteen supporting pairs. Original query and reference DNA were translated back to the reported peptide alignments and matched exactly for every retained HSP. The four local searches each took about one second, excluding database preparation.</p><p><a href="results/common-reference-case-audit-v2/best-locus-alignments.tsv">Per-mate local coordinate evidence</a> · <a href="results/common-reference-case-audit-v2/locus-summary.json">Commands, input hashes and interval summary</a></p>
<h2>Matched protein-only panel</h2><p>A frozen diagnostic panel contains one protein-only candidate pair per sample, absent from both NT24 and NT20, plus 32 shared controls. Among the 200 additions, megablast has viral-only evidence for 73, sensitive blastn for 132 and NR for 129. All 59 megablast-unaligned pairs rescued as viral-only by sensitive blastn also have viral-only NR evidence. Three pairs have viral-only NR evidence but competing nonviral evidence under blastn: SRR33038019_p1346, SRR38294105_p1807 and SRR38294706_p408. These disagreements require inspection; NR is not a truth label. The panel is sample-balanced and outcome-selected, not a read-weighted prevalence estimate.</p>
<h2>Competing matches and comparison limits</h2><p>129,099 candidate pairs have viral plus artificial-sequence competition only. 50,378 have some cellular competition. The latter categories are descriptive and can overlap other categories; artificial sequences must not be described as cellular matches. Primary viral-only/mixed classifications were not changed to improve the counts.</p>
<p>The baseline reads have not all been subjected to the identical NR-all-candidates requirement. Therefore the 156,320 baseline pairs missing from the NR-viral-only protein set are not a measured sensitivity loss. Species assignments, database composition, candidate generation and validation requirements differ. Taxonomy names and human annotations come from the frozen inputs and do not establish infection.</p>
<h2>Measured search cost</h2>
{table(['Method','Query mates','Wall time','Allocated search cost','Cost/query'],[['megablast / core_nt',43,'33.1 s','$0.03344','$0.000778'],['blastn / core_nt',43,'229.4 s','$0.23173','$0.005389'],['tblastx / core_nt',43,'11,408 s','$11.52366','$0.267992'],['DIAMOND / NR',817744,'51,542 s','$37.95793','$0.0000464']])}
<p>Observed TBLASTX cost per query is about 5,773 times pooled DIAMOND/NR, and its matched 43-query wall time is about 49.7 times sensitive blastn. The NR comparison has different batch size, hardware and reference, so this is not an isolated algorithm-speed ratio or a linear projection to a full dataset. No larger default-TBLASTX panel is justified by this pilot; it will not be scaled. A separately bounded faster translated-query pilot uses the same 43 queries and unchanged core_nt.</p>
<p>NR comprises 1.172 billion sequences and 441.88 billion amino acids; its index occupies 918.57 GB. The raw output contains 2,392,256,108 alignment rows and 153.05 GB. core_nt contains 131,855,990 sequences and 1.089 trillion nucleotides; its frozen files occupy 318.55 GB. Search allocations exclude reference build/staging and idle time; those belong in the separate lifetime spend ledger. Commands and timing details are preserved in the search protocol files and completed-search-cost-comparison.json.</p>
<h2>Verification and evidence</h2><p>The original full-file Python aggregation and optimized query-call aggregation produce byte-identical classifications for all 406,173 pooled candidate pairs: SHA-256 <code>{parity['sha256']}</code>. The raw NR output and compact query evidence were downloaded from private S3 and independently hashed. The optimized representation does not replace the preserved raw alignments.</p>
<p><a href="results/protein200-compacted-nt20/pair-evidence.tsv">Per-pair evidence</a> · <a href="results/protein-resolution-compacted-v2/resolution.tsv">Species resolution</a> · <a href="results/protein-similarity-support-compacted-v2-nt20/sample-species-calls.tsv">Similarity support calls</a> · <a href="results/protein-species-gain-cases-compacted-v2/pairs.tsv">Audited original read IDs</a> · <a href="results/protein-species-gain-cases-compacted-v2/alignments.tsv">Audited alignments</a> · <a href="first-step-result.html">Finished upstream-only experiment</a></p>
<p>The already-planned standalone NR seed-panel run is a separate query-context reproducibility check; its result is pending. Production alignment-coordinate duplicate handling and matched validation of all baseline candidates would be needed before claiming production end-to-end call parity or sensitivity.</p></body></html>'''
(r/'nr200-result.html').write_text(text)
print(r/'nr200-result.html')
